[SIZE=2][I]Part 4 of 4. Sections: 10. Conclusion[/I][/SIZE]

[SIZE=5][B]10. Conclusion[/B][/SIZE]

[B]Revisiting section 2's prediction[/B]

We can now grade that original derivation.

[B]The mechanism holds completely.[/B] "Stratum ratios differ enormously between locations, so we can apply extra per-location corrections to covertly emulate stratum control" — correct, and the foundation of the whole system. Section 3's arithmetic verified it: three modifiers really did produce four distinct effective multipliers. The distillation of "change location-level income = expenditure into country-level income = expenditure" was also accurate; that is exactly [ICODE]M x = t[/ICODE].

[B]The feasibility criterion was wrong.[/B] The conclusion at the time was "extracting as few as 4 or 5 linearly independent locations should be enough to solve for a set of coefficients". Linear independence does guarantee a unique solution exists — in R^K. The engine wants a solution in the [I]nonnegative[/I] orthant, and those are far apart: in section 4's example the three columns are fully linearly independent and the solution is unique, yet it is (6.30, 5.24, -23.38), whose third component is deeply negative and therefore useless.

The real criterion is cone containment, and by it 87.3% of real states have no solution. [B]That gap is the source of all the engineering that followed[/B]: had the original prediction been right, this post would have ended at section 4.

Worth adding that the original derivation also contained a [B]correct instinct[/B] that went unexplored: the remark that "severity depends on the power distribution across locations and the relative counts of each location type". That was already brushing against the fact that feasibility depends on a country's internal structure — and section 6's bucketed data (10.1% to 62.5%) is its quantitative form.

[B]Three lessons[/B]

[B]Constraints breed structure.[/B] Had the engine simply provided four per-stratum modifiers, the discovery that location heterogeneity can reconstruct the stratum signal would never have happened.

[B]"Has a solution" and "the solution is usable" are two different things.[/B] This is where the original prediction failed, and where diagnosis kept failing afterwards — attributing infeasibility to poor conditioning, to clamped zeros, to classifier churn; four attributions, all refuted by data. Feasibility is a geometric question (is the target inside the cone), not a numerical one.

[B]The objective function must align with the evaluation criterion.[/B] The four L2 variants and [ICODE]proportional_fast[/ICODE] were not wrongly implemented; their [B]optimisation direction did not match the final evaluation criterion[/B]. L2 lets the large stratum dominate, and [ICODE]proportional_fast[/ICODE] almost never produced a candidate able to pass the hard constraint (4.2% versus the vertex sweep's 90.7%). However correctly they ran, they ran toward the wrong thing. The eventual approach was to define "better" precisely first (scaled L1, plus Linf tie-break, plus hard non-worsening), then go looking for an algorithm.

[B]Closing[/B]

When SOL started, the goal was to make class actually matter economically. The engine offered one scalar modifier. It looked impossible.

Recast mathematically, the impossible became [B]a nonnegative optimisation problem with 4 constraints and K degrees of freedom[/B]. Then the data said: 87.3% of the time, that problem [B]has no exact solution[/B].

But "no exact solution" is not "no answer". Change the goal from "hit the target" to "get as close as possible while guaranteeing no degradation" and the problem becomes solvable — and solves rather well: among exact-failed countries the vertex sweep found an adoptable improvement for 90.7%, dropping median scaled L1 from raw's 0.343 to 0.114.

Players won't notice the relative residual that remains. They will notice nobles and peasants heading toward different fates.

That's enough.

[HR][/HR]

[SPOILER="Appendix A: notation reference"]

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Symbol[/TH][TH]Meaning[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]n[/TD][TD]Number of owned locations[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]K[/TD][TD]Number of classifier classes (fixed at 4)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]S[/TD][TD]Stratum set: nobles, clergy, burghers, lower[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]I_s(l)[/TD][TD]Net income of stratum s at location l[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]B_s(l)[/TD][TD]Base spending of stratum s at location l[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]m_raw(l)[/TD][TD]Raw demand coefficient of location l[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]M[/TD][TD]System matrix, 4 x K (rows are strata, columns are classes)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]t[/TD][TD]Target vector, length 4 (per-stratum liquid funds)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]x[/TD][TD]Class correction factors, length K[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]cone(M)[/TD][TD]Nonnegative cone spanned by M's columns[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]L1, Linf[/TD][TD]Scaled mean and scaled worst-row relative deviation[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]kappa(M)[/TD][TD]Matrix condition number[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]NNLS[/TD][TD]Nonnegative least squares[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

